.TH SET_UNION 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_union \- Compute the union of two sets and store the result

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_union(set_t* out, const set_t* a, const set_t* b);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_union\fR function computes the union of two sets and stores the result. It takes three arguments: the set to store the result, and the two sets to compute the union of. The function returns the number of elements in the result.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB out
The set to store the result.
.TP
.IB a
The first set to compute the union of.
.TP
.IB b
The second set to compute the union of.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns the number of elements in the result.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_union\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set_a, set_b, set_c;
    set_init(&set_a);
    set_init(&set_b);
    set_init(&set_c);

    set_inserts(&set_a, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set_a, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set_a, "cherry");

    set_inserts(&set_b, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set_b, "cherry");
    set_inserts(&set_b, "date");

    printf("Set A: %u elements\n", set_a.len);
    printf("Set B: %u elements\n", set_b.len);

    int r = set_union(&set_c, &set_a, &set_b);

    printf("Set C (result of A ∪ B): %u elements\n", set_c.len);

    set_free(&set_a);
    set_free(&set_b);
    set_free(&set_c);
    return 0;
}
.nef